description_block:
Hall of Famer, A 5-piece collection of baseball subjects from the 1911 T3 Turkey Red Cabinets release is presented. The majestic "Turkey Red" cabinets, made available by the manufacturer only as select premiums in exchange for special coupons, stand among the most beautiful and impressive collectibles in the vintage hobby. The offered array holds specimens which—while maintaining generally favorable image quality (with largely routine peripheral wear and other injuries) from an obverse perspective—exhibit reverse-side scarring and paper loss from one-time mounting (and apparently hasty, subsequent removal from that environment). Cardbacks feature the production's "Checklist Back (Offer)" design (stating the issue's redemption terms and lists itemizing T3 and T9 subjects). Includes:
#13 Larry Doyle, N.Y. Nat'l - generally clean surfaces on both sides, corner rounding;
#40 Vic Willis, St. Louis Nat'l - clean image, clipped corners, scuffing in margins, back damage, top-margin puncture;
#41 Hooks Wiltse, N.Y. Nat'l - clean image, clipped corners, scuffing in margins, back damage, top-margin puncture;
#45 Jordan & Herzog At First - 2-player action scene, generally clean surfaces on both sides, corner rounding, large, torn-open puncture at top edge with associated small holes, creases;92 Jimmy Dygert, Phila. Amer. - generally clean surfaces on both sides, corner rounding, some edge/corner chipping.
Display quality (with note to details as described) occupies a wide range, about PR to VG/EX.
Please note that this offering does not contain a complete set. Potential bidders are encouraged to ask questions prior to bidding, at least 72 hours prior to auction close. The sale of this lot is on an "as-is" basis, and the sale is final.
Please note: There are raw items contained in this lot, which means they are ungraded and not encased in protective slabs. While we take efforts to ensure that the items are as described, they are sold in their raw state and may exhibit typical signs of wear such as minor imperfections, creases, discoloration, or slight damage. The images provided represent a portion of the item; however, buyers should be aware that they are purchasing based on the item's title, images available, and description, not the condition. THIS ITEM IS SOLD ON AN AS-IS BASIS, and all sales are final and made in accordance with Goldin's User Agreement; provided that this disclaimer shall govern in the event of any express discrepancy as between this disclaimer and the Conditions of Sale.

Presented is a 72-piece complete set of 1941 Play Ball baseball cards. Each card has been graded by PSA. Throughout a gathering loaded with Hall of Fame content—including key subjects Joe DiMaggio, Ted Williams, Hank Greenberg, and Jimmie Foxx—these treasured cards are distinguished by beautiful pastel hues. One could never have realized during the time of these superstar depictions' initial distribution, which saw them all revealed in exquisite detail and dazzling color with each surrounded by a white to off-white frame, that this would be the final baseball card release before the virtual halt in production of cardboard collectibles due to the onset of World War II.
The three consecutive years of Gum, Inc.'s widely appreciated "Play Ball" efforts concluded a second renaissance of sorts in the realm of baseball-themed trading cards. The specialty originally came into being and flourished for a time during the "Tobacco Wars" controversies, roughly 1890s and 1910s. The 1930s, of course, saw the emergence and growth of Goudey ("Big League Gum!") and its competitors; this period threatened to pass quickly, largely under the weight of oppressive emotional and financial tolls taken by the Great Depression. The advantage briefly held by Goudey also eroded in the face of advances by J. Warren Bowman, the entrepreneur responsible for Blony bubble gum. With the dominant gum product in hand, Bowman's Gum, Inc. soon trained its sights on the card market, too. The success of various Gum, Inc. non-sport releases, like 1938's classic "Horrors of War," opened the door for a distinctive 1939 "Play Ball" baseball issue, and an even nicer, subsequent array under that brand in 1940. Then, this beautifully refined 1941 "Play Ball" gallery arrived to became, in fact, this era's "grand finale" for the topic, in particular, and for the cartophilic hobby, in general.
This thoroughly appealing, historically important full set includes: Graded
-PSA NM 7: 2 cards w/#'s 5 (MC), 53;
-PSA EX-MT+ 6.5: 1 card, #18 Greenberg;
-PSA EX-MT 6: 1 card, #56;
-PSA EX 5: 6 cards w/#'s 7, 12, 15 Cronin, 22, 41, 42;
-PSA VG-EX+ 4.5: 7 cards w/#'s 2, 4, 11, 17, 27, 36, 63 D. DiMaggio;
-PSA VG-EX 4: 27 cards w/#'s 1 Miller, 9, 10 Vaughan, 21, 24, 25, 26, 28, 34, 37, 39, 40, 43, 44, 46, 47, 48, 51 (MC), 52, 54 Reese, 58, 59, 61 V. DiMaggio, 62, 65, 67, 72 Gomez;
-PSA VG+ 3.5: 5 cards w/#'s 6 Hubbell, 31, 45, 68, 70 Dickey;
-PSA VG 3: 9 cards w/#'s 3, 16 (MC), 32, 33, 50, 55, 57, 66, 69;
-PSA GD+ 2.5: 3 cards w/#'s 13 Foxx, 20 Ruffing, 35;
-PSA GD 2: 7 cards w/#'s 19 Gehringer, 23, 30, 49, 60 Klein, 64 Doerr, 71 J. DiMaggio;
-PSA FR 1.5: 2 cards w/#'s 8 Ott, 29;
-PSA PR 1: 2 cards w/#'s 14 Williams, 38.
Overall condition is mostly VG to EX or finer (some lesser).
Please note that potential bidders are encouraged to ask questions prior to bidding, at least 72 hours prior to auction close. The sale of this lot is on an "as-is" basis, and the sale is final.
